IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `er4-nouns`
IMPORT `er4-part3-reg63-weekly-indemnity-plan`
IMPORT `er4-part3-regs64-66-other-plans`
IMPORT `er4-part3-qualifying-plan`
IMPORT `er4-part3-application`
IMPORT `er4-part3-1-entitlement`
IMPORT `er4-part3-1-premiums-and-administration`
IMPORT `er4-seat`

-- Worked cases for the seat layer er4-seat.l4: at least one case for each of its 45 questions, in the facts a real person in
-- the seat would state. Every expected value is written by hand from the text of the Regulations (SOR-96-332.txt, lines
-- 993-1393), never copied from the output. Where a question serves both the employed ("insured") person and the self-employed
-- person, there are MIRROR CASES: the same facts asked from both seats, with answers that agree. Dates, amounts and the Act's
-- figures (35, 61, 40, ...) are invented inputs for the tests and are not asserted as law.

§ `Part II -- the national employment service`

-- Priya has just been laid off and wants to use the national employment service. She is a worker.
#ASSERT `Can I use the national employment service at no cost` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Someone who neither works nor looks for work is not named by reg 59.
#ASSERT `Can I use the national employment service at no cost` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to make labour market information available to me` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to make labour market information available to me` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- (c)(i): job openings and careers in demand, for every worker.
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to give me information on job openings and on careers in demand` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the national employment service to give me information on job openings and on careers in demand` FALSE
-- (c)(ii): referral to other sources, where the service considers it appropriate.
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to refer me to other sources of labour market information` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the national employment service to refer me to other sources of labour market information` TRUE FALSE
-- (c)(iii): the service may obtain the information from a worker seeking employment.
#ASSERT `May the national employment service obtain from me the information it needs to refer me to jobs` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `May the national employment service obtain from me the information it needs to refer me to jobs` TRUE FALSE
-- (c)(iv): interview and counselling needs both the local strategies and necessity.
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to interview and counsel me and help me make an action plan` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the national employment service to interview and counsel me and help me make an action plan` TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- (c)(v): special assistance, where the worker has particular difficulty obtaining or keeping employment.
#ASSERT `Is the national employment service to tell me about special assistance` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the national employment service to tell me about special assistance` TRUE FALSE

§ `Part III -- my employer's sick-leave or wage-loss plan`

-- Chen is covered by their employer's weekly indemnity plan, which meets its standards and is a qualifying plan: the rate is reduced.
#ASSERT `Is my employer's premium rate reduced because of me` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- Reg 62(2)(c): if Chen's eligibility to use paid sick leave were deferred (a temporary employee under a paid sick leave plan), no reduction.
#ASSERT NOT `Is my employer's premium rate reduced because of me` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my employer's premium rate reduced because of me` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- Reg 61: insured persons must benefit by at least five-twelfths of a reduction of 1,200 dollars: 500.
#ASSERT `By how much must the insured persons benefit from my employer's premium reduction` 1200 EQUALS 500
#ASSERT `Does the Part on employers' wage-loss plans apply to me and my co-workers` TRUE 1200 500
#ASSERT NOT `Does the Part on employers' wage-loss plans apply to me and my co-workers` TRUE 1200 499

§§ `Reg 63 to 66 -- does my employer's plan meet the standards for its kind?`

-- Helper for the cases only: every fact of the 26 is fixed at a plan that meets reg 63 (a weekly indemnity plan, 550 of 1,000 a week, eligible on
-- 1 May 2026 for employment begun 15 January 2026, 7-day elimination period, 3 months to reinstate on a recurrence, 15 weeks of benefit);
-- a case varies the facts it is about. The nine facts that matter to 64, 65 and 66 are the parameters.
GIVEN kind IS A `er4 Kind of wage-loss plan`
      benefit IS A NUMBER
      newill IS A NUMBER
      weeks IS A NUMBER
      exhaustion IS A BOOLEAN
      employer IS A `er4 Employer that provides or finances a plan`
      credited IS A NUMBER
      reserved IS A NUMBER
      deferred IS A NUMBER
      accumulates IS A BOOLEAN
      maxdays IS A NUMBER
      workdays IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`er4 test plan answer` MEANS
    `Does my employer's wage-loss plan meet the standards for its kind`
        kind FALSE (YMD 2026 1 15) (YMD 2026 5 1) (YMD 2026 3 20) TRUE 7 TRUE TRUE TRUE benefit 1000 3 newill weeks exhaustion FALSE FALSE
        employer TRUE credited reserved deferred accumulates maxdays workdays

-- Ahmed's weekly indemnity plan pays 550 of 1,000 a week (55%), reinstates on a recurrence after 3 months, pays 15 weeks: reg 63 is met.
#ASSERT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` 550 1 15 FALSE `er4 any other employer` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
-- At 549 a week it is under 55%: not met (63(d)).
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` 549 1 15 FALSE `er4 any other employer` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
-- A weekly indemnity plan that stops paying when accumulated sick leave runs out has an event reg 63(f) does not name.
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
-- A special weekly indemnity plan of a municipal authority: one month to reinstate, 52 weeks of benefit.
#ASSERT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Special Weekly Indemnity Plan` 550 1 52 FALSE `er4 a municipal authority` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Special Weekly Indemnity Plan` 550 1 51 FALSE `er4 a municipal authority` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Special Weekly Indemnity Plan` 550 1 52 FALSE `er4 any other employer` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0
-- A cumulative paid sick leave plan: one day credited and one reserved a month, 75 working days accumulated and paid, exhaustion allowed.
#ASSERT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 1 1 12 TRUE 75 75
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 1 1 12 TRUE 74 75
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 1 1 13 TRUE 75 75
-- An enhanced plan needs 1 2/3 days credited and reserved, and 125 working days.
#ASSERT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 an Enhanced Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 2 2 0 TRUE 125 125
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 an Enhanced Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` 550 1 15 TRUE `er4 any other employer` 1.5 1.5 0 TRUE 125 125
-- A plan of none of the four kinds cannot meet any of regs 63 to 66.
#ASSERT NOT `er4 test plan answer` `er4 another wage-loss plan` 550 1 15 FALSE `er4 any other employer` 0 0 0 FALSE 0 0

§§ `Reg 67, 69 and 71 -- category, qualifying plan, dates`

-- Reg 69(1): a person under a weekly indemnity plan that satisfies reg 63 is in category (a); under a cumulative plan and deferred, in (e).
#ASSERT `Which category of insured persons am I placed in on my employer's application` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `er4 insured persons covered by a plan that satisfies the requirements of section 63`
#ASSERT `Which category of insured persons am I placed in on my employer's application` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `er4 all other insured persons, including those referred to in paragraph 65(d)`
-- Reg 71: an initial application on 16 March is after the 15th, so the reduction takes effect on 1 May; on 15 March, on 1 April.
#ASSERT `From what date does my employer's premium reduction take effect` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 16) (YMD 2026 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2026 5 1
#ASSERT `From what date does my employer's premium reduction take effect` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 15) (YMD 2026 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2026 4 1
-- A continuation: the date the plan was modified.
#ASSERT `From what date does my employer's premium reduction take effect` FALSE (YMD 2026 3 16) (YMD 2026 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2026 9 1
-- Reg 67(3): written commitment, meets its section, no (5) condition for a weekly plan.
#ASSERT `Is my employer's plan a qualifying plan` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my employer's plan a qualifying plan` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- Only a board resolution that was never implemented is no commitment at all.
#ASSERT NOT `Is my employer's plan a qualifying plan` `er4 a Weekly Indemnity Plan` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- A paid sick leave plan must also be credited as 67(5) says.
#ASSERT `Is my employer's plan a qualifying plan` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my employer's plan a qualifying plan` `er4 a Cumulative Paid Sick Leave Plan` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- Reg 67(2): the plan became a qualifying plan on 10 April 2026 (considered 1 April by 67(4)); the first application was on 10 March,
-- effective 1 April (71): April to December, 9 months.
#ASSERT `For how many months of the year does my employer's premium reduction apply` 2026 (YMD 2026 4 10) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) TRUE (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS 9
-- Applied on 20 April: effective 1 June by reg 71, so June to December, 7 months.
#ASSERT `For how many months of the year does my employer's premium reduction apply` 2026 (YMD 2026 4 10) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) TRUE (YMD 2026 4 20) (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS 7
-- And the plan stopped being a qualifying plan on 20 September 2026 (considered 1 October): April to September, 6 months.
#ASSERT `For how many months of the year does my employer's premium reduction apply` 2026 (YMD 2026 4 10) TRUE (YMD 2026 9 20) TRUE (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS 6

§ `Parts III.1 and III.2 -- am I barred from federal benefits because I can receive provincial benefits?`

-- Benefits paid to Marie under her province's plan because of her pregnancy are provincial benefits; her cousin's, paid for a house move, are not.
#ASSERT `Are the benefits I am paid provincial benefits within the meaning of these Regulations` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Are the benefits I am paid provincial benefits within the meaning of these Regulations` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Mirror: a self-employed person paid for the care of a new-born.
#ASSERT `Are the benefits I am paid provincial benefits within the meaning of these Regulations` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my province's plan a plan to which these Regulations apply` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my province's plan a plan to which these Regulations apply` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my province's plan a plan to which these Regulations apply` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- Reg 76.09(1): Marie is entitled to provincial benefits and has not asked for a determination: disentitled to maternity and parental benefits.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- Reg 76.09(2): she asked, and the Commission found the provincial amount not substantially equivalent: (1) does not apply.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- Asking is not enough; the Commission must have found it.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- Not entitled to provincial benefits: not barred.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Mirror cases, the same facts as a self-employed person (reg 76.36(1), (2)): the answers agree.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled to maternity or parental benefits because I can receive provincial benefits` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Reg 76.09(4) and 76.36(4): moving afterwards makes no difference to someone who applied and is entitled.
#ASSERT `Am I still caught by the provincial benefit rules if I have moved to another province` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I still caught by the provincial benefit rules if I have moved to another province` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I still caught by the provincial benefit rules if I have moved to another province` TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- Reg 76.09(3): a week of other benefits (or of fishing benefits) is barred for a week of provincial benefits.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to a week of other benefits, or of fishing benefits, because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 other benefits under Part I of the Act` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to a week of other benefits, or of fishing benefits, because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 benefits under the Employment Insurance (Fishing) Regulations` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to a week of other benefits, or of fishing benefits, because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 other benefits under Part I of the Act` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Maternity and parental benefits are outside (3); (1) governs them.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to a week of other benefits, or of fishing benefits, because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 benefits under section 23 of the Act` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled to a week of other benefits, or of fishing benefits, because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 benefits of another kind` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Mirror: a self-employed person's week of other Part VII.1 benefits (reg 76.36(3)).
#ASSERT `As a self-employed person, am I disentitled to a week of other benefits because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 other benefits under Part VII.1 of the Act` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `As a self-employed person, am I disentitled to a week of other benefits because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 benefits under section 152.05 of the Act` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `As a self-employed person, am I disentitled to a week of other benefits because I received provincial benefits for that week` `er4 benefits of another kind` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Reg 76.09(5): Dev is caring for their child with Sam, who has applied for provincial benefits and is entitled to them.
-- Arguments: I am self-employed, the other person is, same child, the other person entitled, I asked the Commission, it found.
-- Both insured (76.09(5)(a)): Dev is brought under the bars.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- Dev asks the Commission and it finds the provincial amount not substantially equivalent: no bar.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- Not caring for the same child, or the other person not entitled: no bar.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Mirror cases by who is self-employed: Dev self-employed, Sam insured (76.09(5)(b)); Dev insured, Sam self-employed (76.36(5)(b));
-- both self-employed (76.36(5)(a)). The answers agree.
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I disentitled because the other person caring for our child is entitled to provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE

§ `Parts III.1 and III.2 -- how provincial benefits affect my claim and my weeks`

-- Reg 76.11: Lena's provincial benefit period ended 1 July 2021 and she asks for assistance on 30 June 2026 (within 60 months), unemployed, and
-- would have had special benefits: she is an insured participant. Arguments: already, requests, unemployed, has a benefit period, ended on, has ended, request day, would have.
#ASSERT `Am I an insured participant for employment benefits assistance` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE (YMD 2021 7 1) TRUE (YMD 2026 6 30) TRUE
-- One day beyond 60 months: not.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I an insured participant for employment benefits assistance` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE (YMD 2021 6 29) TRUE (YMD 2026 6 30) TRUE
-- A current provincial benefit period suffices.
#ASSERT `Am I an insured participant for employment benefits assistance` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 6 30) TRUE
-- Already an insured participant under s 58.
#ASSERT `Am I an insured participant for employment benefits assistance` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 6 30) FALSE
-- Reg 76.13: a week of provincial benefits counts as a week in receipt of benefits.
#ASSERT `Does a week I was paid provincial benefits count as a week in receipt of benefits when my qualifying period is extended` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does a week I was paid provincial benefits count as a week in receipt of benefits when my qualifying period is extended` FALSE FALSE
-- Reg 76.14(a): provincial benefits for pregnancy count as benefits for a 12(3)(a) reason; provincial benefits cannot count for sickness.
#ASSERT `Do my benefits count as paid for pregnancy or the care of a new-born when my benefit period is extended` `pregnancy` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Do my benefits count as paid for pregnancy or the care of a new-born when my benefit period is extended` `a prescribed illness, injury or quarantine` FALSE TRUE
-- Reg 76.14(b): for sickness, up to the applicable maximum (26, from s 12(3)(c)), not the total of 50.
#ASSERT `Up to how many weeks is my benefit period extended so that my other special benefits can be paid` `a prescribed illness, injury or quarantine` 26 50 EQUALS 26
#ASSERT `Up to how many weeks is my benefit period extended so that my other special benefits can be paid` `care or support of a critically ill adult` 15 50 EQUALS 15
-- Reg 76.15: Tomas's child spent 3 weeks in hospital in the period; he is entitled to provincial benefits.
#ASSERT `By how many weeks is my benefit period extended because my child was in hospital` TRUE 3 EQUALS JUST 3
#ASSERT `By how many weeks is my benefit period extended because my child was in hospital` FALSE 3 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Regs 76.16 and 76.17: 400 dollars payable for a week, 150 of provincial benefits for the same week: 250. Mirror for the self-employed (76.37, 76.38).
#ASSERT `How much will my benefits for a week be after the provincial benefits are deducted` FALSE TRUE 400 150 EQUALS JUST 250
#ASSERT `How much will my benefits for a week be after the provincial benefits are deducted` TRUE TRUE 400 150 EQUALS JUST 250
-- No provincial benefits for the week: this regulation does not reduce it.
#ASSERT `How much will my benefits for a week be after the provincial benefits are deducted` FALSE FALSE 400 150 EQUALS JUST 400
-- A deduction larger than what is payable is not answered by the text.
#ASSERT `How much will my benefits for a week be after the provincial benefits are deducted` FALSE TRUE 100 150 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Regs 76.18 and 76.39: a week of provincial benefits already counted for a purpose excludes a week of EI benefits for the same purpose; mirror for the self-employed.
#ASSERT NOT `May a week of my EI benefits be counted for a purpose when a week of my provincial benefits has already been counted for it` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `May a week of my EI benefits be counted for a purpose when a week of my provincial benefits has already been counted for it` FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `May a week of my EI benefits be counted for a purpose when a week of my provincial benefits has already been counted for it` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `May a week of my EI benefits be counted for a purpose when a week of my provincial benefits has already been counted for it` TRUE FALSE
-- Regs 76.19(1) and 76.4(1): 20 weeks paid under the Act and 10 weeks of provincial benefits against an overall maximum of 50: 20 left.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits are left within my maximum once my provincial weeks are counted` FALSE 50 20 10 TRUE EQUALS JUST 20
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits are left within my maximum once my provincial weeks are counted` TRUE 50 20 10 TRUE EQUALS JUST 20
-- If she would not have been entitled to the corresponding benefits, the provincial weeks do not count: 30 left.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits are left within my maximum once my provincial weeks are counted` FALSE 50 20 10 FALSE EQUALS JUST 30
-- Regs 76.19(2), (3) and 76.4(3), (4): 10 weeks paid at an accelerated rate (35 weeks at the usual rate, 25 at the accelerated): 14 weeks.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits do my provincial weeks, paid at an accelerated rate, represent` FALSE TRUE 10 35 25 EQUALS JUST 14
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits do my provincial weeks, paid at an accelerated rate, represent` TRUE TRUE 10 35 25 EQUALS JUST 14
-- A ratio of 15/10: 5 weeks are 7.5, rounded up to 8.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits do my provincial weeks, paid at an accelerated rate, represent` FALSE TRUE 5 15 10 EQUALS JUST 8
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits do my provincial weeks, paid at an accelerated rate, represent` TRUE TRUE 5 15 10 EQUALS JUST 8
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits do my provincial weeks, paid at an accelerated rate, represent` FALSE FALSE 7 35 25 EQUALS JUST 7
-- Regs 76.2 and 76.41: the Act allows 35 weeks, the province 40; 38 paid: 35 taken into account.
#ASSERT `How many of my provincial weeks are taken into account when the provincial plan allows more weeks than the Act` FALSE 35 40 38 EQUALS 35
#ASSERT `How many of my provincial weeks are taken into account when the provincial plan allows more weeks than the Act` TRUE 35 40 38 EQUALS 35
#ASSERT `How many of my provincial weeks are taken into account when the provincial plan allows more weeks than the Act` FALSE 40 40 38 EQUALS 38
-- Regs 76.19(1.1) and 76.4(2): a benefit period under a provincial law is deemed to have begun the same week.
#ASSERT `When is my benefit period deemed to have begun when I had a benefit period under a provincial law` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) TRUE EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 3 1)
#ASSERT `When is my benefit period deemed to have begun when I had a benefit period under a provincial law` TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) TRUE EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 3 1)
#ASSERT `When is my benefit period deemed to have begun when I had a benefit period under a provincial law` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Regs 76.21 and 76.42. Arguments: self-employed, same child, different provinces, weeks otherwise, other parent applied first, agreement or order, election, A, B, C, D.
-- Insured: 35 weeks otherwise, Nia applied first (odd: 1 + 17 = 18), cap 40 - 15 = 25, ceiling 35: 18.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 35 FALSE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS JUST 18
-- The other parent applied first: (35 - 1)/2 = 17.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 35 TRUE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS JUST 17
-- Mirror: the same facts for a self-employed person (reg 76.42): the answers agree.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE TRUE 35 FALSE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS JUST 18
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` TRUE TRUE TRUE 35 TRUE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS JUST 17
-- An even number, 36: half, 18, whoever applied first.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 36 TRUE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS JUST 18
-- The cap binds: 30 weeks of provincial benefits paid to the other parent leave 40 - 30 = 10.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 35 FALSE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 30 61 35 EQUALS JUST 10
-- The 61-week election: share 30, cap floor(10 x 61/35) = 17.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 61 TRUE FALSE `the 61-week maximum` 40 30 61 35 EQUALS JUST 17
-- An agreement or a court order: the Regulations give no number. Not in different provinces, or not the same child: (2) does not apply.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE TRUE 35 TRUE TRUE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` FALSE TRUE FALSE 35 TRUE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `How many weeks of parental benefits can I be paid when the other parent claims provincial benefits` TRUE FALSE TRUE 35 TRUE FALSE `the 35-week maximum` 40 15 61 35 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Regs 76.22 and 76.421: provincial benefits were paid, so the waiting period is waived; mirror for the self-employed.
#ASSERT `Is my waiting period waived because provincial benefits were paid to me` FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `Is my waiting period waived because provincial benefits were paid to me` TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `Is my waiting period waived because provincial benefits were paid to me` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my waiting period waived because provincial benefits were paid to me` FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Is my waiting period waived because provincial benefits were paid to me` TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- Reg 76.23. Part III.1's coming-into-force day is not in the text; 1 January 2020 is invented. A child born 31 December 2019: the old Act applies.
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it stood before the provincial plan rules still apply to my claim` (YMD 2019 12 31) (YMD 2020 1 1) 0
-- Born after, nothing paid before: no. Born after but already paid two weeks before: yes.
#ASSERT NOT `Does the Act as it stood before the provincial plan rules still apply to my claim` (YMD 2020 3 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) 0
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it stood before the provincial plan rules still apply to my claim` (YMD 2020 3 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) 2
-- Reg 76.43: Olga ceased to reside in the province on 15 June 2026 and made the agreement exactly 12 months before.
#ASSERT `Am I deemed to meet the 12-month condition after moving out of a province with a plan` (YMD 2026 6 15) (YMD 2025 6 15) TRUE
-- Three months after (15 September 2026) is within it; a day later is not.
#ASSERT `Am I deemed to meet the 12-month condition after moving out of a province with a plan` (YMD 2026 6 15) (YMD 2026 9 15) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I deemed to meet the 12-month condition after moving out of a province with a plan` (YMD 2026 6 15) (YMD 2026 9 16) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I deemed to meet the 12-month condition after moving out of a province with a plan` (YMD 2026 6 15) (YMD 2025 6 15) FALSE

§ `Parts III.1 and III.2 -- the premium I pay`

-- Reg 76.24: Jean is covered by her province's plan but works in a province with none: no reduction at source.
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 in a province that has no provincial plan` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `er4 the employer does not take the premium reduction into account`
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 outside Canada` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `er4 the employer does not take the premium reduction into account`
-- Reg 76.25: Karim works in a province with a plan but is not covered by it: the reduction is taken into account.
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 in a province that has a provincial plan` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `er4 the employer takes the premium reduction into account`
-- Covered and working in the plan's province (the ordinary case of reg 76.04): taken into account if the effect in 69(2) holds.
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 in a province that has a provincial plan` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `er4 the employer takes the premium reduction into account`
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 in a province that has a provincial plan` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `er4 the Regulations do not say`
-- Neither covered nor working in a province with a plan: none of 76.04, 76.24, 76.25 speaks.
#ASSERT `Will my employer take the provincial premium reduction into account when deducting my premium` `er4 in a province that has no provincial plan` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `er4 the Regulations do not say`
-- Reg 76.33: the self-employment premium is reduced.
#ASSERT `As a self-employed person, is my self-employment premium reduced because of provincial benefits` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `As a self-employed person, is my self-employment premium reduced because of provincial benefits` FALSE
-- Regs 76.05 and 76.34: 40,000 of earnings at a reduction rate of 0.54%: 216 dollars, employed or self-employed.
#ASSERT `By how much is my premium reduced` FALSE 40000 0.0054 EQUALS 216
#ASSERT `By how much is my premium reduced` TRUE 40000 0.0054 EQUALS 216
-- Reg 76.26(1): resident in the province on 31 December; 300 of reduction deducted + 100 overpaid = 400, below the 500 owed: 400; plus 420 remitted by employers.
#ASSERT `How much premium adjustment is paid to my province on my behalf` TRUE 300 100 500 420 EQUALS 820
-- Owing only 150: the lesser, 150, plus 420.
#ASSERT `How much premium adjustment is paid to my province on my behalf` TRUE 300 100 150 420 EQUALS 570
-- Not resident there on 31 December: nothing is paid for her.
#ASSERT `How much premium adjustment is paid to my province on my behalf` FALSE 300 100 500 420 EQUALS 0
-- Reg 76.28: 1,200 deducted and an adjustment of 300: 1,500 counted; a premium adjustment paid to the province is not counted for Part IV.
#ASSERT `What premiums are counted in deciding whether I overpaid, once my province's premium adjustment is added` 1200 300 EQUALS 1500
#ASSERT NOT `Is a premium adjustment paid to my province counted in deciding whether I overpaid` TRUE
#ASSERT `Is a premium adjustment paid to my province counted in deciding whether I overpaid` FALSE
